# 7. A WEBSITE WHERE EVERY SECTION MOVES

The entire website must share the same cinematic visual language.

Each section should feel like a new environment within one continuous world.

The 3D canvas should remain persistent where practical.

HTML content should remain accessible and readable above the canvas.

As the visitor scrolls, animate the camera, objects, lighting and composition.

Do not animate every object at the same speed.

Different objects should have different movement patterns based on their distance from the camera, physical role and position in the composition.

Use the following motion principles:

### Camera movement

Use controlled dolly movements, lateral tracking, subtle orbits and carefully planned transitions.

### Object movement

Objects should rotate, translate, separate, assemble and change position according to the section narrative.

### Parallax

Foreground objects should move more noticeably than background objects.

### Lighting

Lighting should change naturally between environments.

The dark architectural hero should transition into cooler scientific lighting and eventually warmer library lighting.

### Depth

Use realistic scale relationships, perspective, occlusion and shadows.

### Timing

Some animations should follow scroll progress directly.

Others can use short, restrained transitions when a section enters view.

### Reversibility

The experience should respond naturally when the visitor scrolls backward.

### Readability

Text and buttons must remain readable even when the 3D environment is moving.

The objective is to create a website that feels like a continuous cinematic journey rather than a series of disconnected animations.

